<p>← LOOKING BACK &nbsp;|&nbsp; LOOKING AHEAD →</p>
<p>You now have the full shape of Aristotle's ethics: a final end (eudaimonia), the virtues that constitute it (acquired through habituation, structured by the mean), and an account of when we are responsible for what we do and who we become. In the next unit, Thomas Aquinas will inherit this picture almost wholesale and add a theological dimension: what happens to virtue ethics when you locate human nature and the human good inside a Christian cosmos?</p>
